=== 3. corollary on integers (the map T itself; window of 5*2^k consecutive integers containing negatives) ===
   k =  0: counts by residue [0, 1, 0, 0, 0]; pentagon law x 2^k [0, 1, 0, 0, 0]; return prob 1
   k =  1: counts by residue [0, 0, 1, 1, 0]; pentagon law x 2^k [0, 0, 1, 1, 0]; return prob 0
   k =  2: counts by residue [1, 2, 0, 0, 1]; pentagon law x 2^k [1, 2, 0, 0, 1]; return prob 1/2
   k =  8: counts by residue [57, 70, 36, 36, 57]; pentagon law x 2^k [57, 70, 36, 36, 57]; return prob 35/128
   k = 16: counts by residue [13380, 13990, 12393, 12393, 13380]; pentagon law x 2^k [13380, 13990, 12393, 12393, 13380]; return prob 6995/32768
   [ok] k = 0..16: law of T^k(n) mod 5 over n = 1 (mod 5) in a window of length 5*2^k = pentagon-walk law; return prob (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   start n = 2 (mod 5), k = 1: counts [0, 2, 0, 0, 0]  (a pentagon walk from a neighbour would split 1/2, 1/2)

